D2. This word is out of place after where. ~ AT
D3. Here's where an Englishman spends Saturday night. ~ TUB
D4. A Frenchman's "very". ~ TRES
D5. Here's where certain ancients used to drown their sorrows. ~ LETHE
D6. The spooky railroad. ~ EERIE
D7. A bum caster. ~ SNARLER
D8. A short able seaman. ~ AS
D9. Antique sud container. ~ KEG
D10. Any movie not a newsreel. ~ EPIC
D11. This runs in Yes-men's veins. ~ SIRUP
D14. What kind of stock is found on the Matterhorn? ~ ALPEN
D15. These are drillers of men. ~ MOSQUITOES
D17. Okay on the Bowery. ~ YEP
D19. Short-time parking places for servant girls. ~ SITUATIONS
D21. A tactful way to make a touch. ~ ROUNDABOUT
D23. The condition of Dumb Dora's cranium. ~ SOFT
D25. The political spellbinder's stock in trade. ~ DETRACTION
D27. This is akin to love but not as satisfactory. ~ PITY
D29. A sticky feather bed. ~ TAR
D32. The knights of old, This knocked 'em cold! ~ TILT
D34. Here's where Scotchmen hoard their ivory (pl.). ~ TAMS
D37. Lamb's pen name. ~ ELIA
D39. This is the best receiver on the market. ~ EAR
D40. Mild socks. ~ PATS
D44. Penrod's baby-talking lady. ~ LOLA
D47. For what does many a motorist walk a mile? ~ GAS
D48. These flew the Atlantic long before Lindbergh. ~ ERNS
D49. A highbrow guess. ~ SURMISE
D52. This is never at the outset. ~ INSET
D56. She calls for threes But really wears these! ~ SIXES
D58. Three strikes. ~ OUT
D59. Why editors cut up. ~ EMEND
D60. To recapture. ~ REBAG
D63. A wet wind. ~ EAST
D65. A good place to do a split. ~ RAIL
D68. This gets them coming and going (abbr.). ~ STA
D70. Ribald Liars Ass'n, you know how. ~ RLA
